=== SUMMARY: Qwen3-30B-A3B Q2_K (2.95 bits), one session, RAM 12.1 GB free, XMP 3000 MT/s ===

  config                                   tg32              tg128
  all experts -> CPU (--no-mmap)      16.87 +/- 0.30    17.44 +/- 0.38  (17.58 +/- 0.91 re-run hot)
  split K=10, 22%->VRAM (tool ships)         -          18.38 +/- 0.63
  split K=16, 33%->VRAM (prereg #13 peak)  18.89 +/- 0.41  18.84 +/- 0.48

  gain of the peak split over the all-CPU baseline:
    at tg32   +12.0%   <- reproduces the published +12.4% under its own protocol
    at tg128   +7.6%   <- the gain SHRINKS with generation length

  The tool's shipped cutoff (K=10) delivers +5.0% at tg128 where the measured optimum (K=16)
  delivers +7.6%. The conservative DESKTOP_VRAM_RESERVE is deliberate (the overcommit cliff is
  -29%), but it leaves ~2.6pp on the table on this box.

  THERMAL: unlike the all-in-VRAM 7B (which decayed 3.8% from 47C to 72C), this placement is
  thermally flat - 17.44 at 58C vs 17.58 at 62C. Expected: it is bound by system RAM, not by
  the GPU, so GPU clock/temperature barely enters.

  OPEN: today's absolute baseline (16.87-17.58) sits ~10% below the historical flagship anchor
  of 19.30 measured on this same box and model class. RAM is not under pressure (12.1 GB free)
  and XMP is still on at 3000 MT/s, so neither of the obvious causes explains it. The law's
  prediction (17.6 hybrid) matches TODAY's measurement, not the historical one. Needs a
  controlled re-measurement against the original file and llama.cpp build before the 19.30
  figure is quoted again.
